            <ArticleTitle>Giant Cell Tumor of the Talus: 19-Year Follow-Up of a Patient.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>958-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1053/j.jfas.2014.04.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1067-2516(14)00139-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Giant cell tumor in small bones is a rare condition characterized by extensive bony destruction and a high recurrence rate. Intralesional excision with curettage and autologous bone grafting has been used as a standard treatment method for giant cell tumor of the bones. We report the case of a 30-year-old female with giant cell tumor of the talar body. She was followed up for 19 years after intralesional curettage and autologous bone grafting treatment.</AbstractText>
